using Global

~ flashback_start

# Setting: Two years ago. Jason's manager's office. A different Jason — sharper, hungrier, no idea yet what "special accounts" means.

Manager: Close the door.

Manager: You've got the best close rate on the floor. I want to give you something bigger.

Manager: A private buyer. Overseas. Pays triple standard commission.

Jason: What's the catch?

Manager: No catch. Just... this one doesn't go through the usual review. Buyer wants discretion, that's all.

- Ask what "discretion" means. [if leverage >= 0] => deal_pressed
- Just take the deal. [if compliance >= 0] => deal_accepted
- Say you're not comfortable with it. => deal_hesitant

~ deal_pressed

Jason: Discretion meaning what, exactly?

Manager: It means the export license is still "in process." It'll clear eventually. Paperwork's slow, not wrong.

Manager: You're not signing anything illegal. You're closing a sale. Compliance handles compliance.

do leverage += 1

=> deal_pivot

~ deal_accepted

Jason: Sounds good. Send me the file.

Manager: Good man. I knew you'd get it.

Manager: Numbers on your desk by Friday. Don't mention this one at the floor meeting.

do compliance += 1

=> deal_pivot

~ deal_hesitant

Jason: I don't know, this doesn't sound like something I should be doing.

Manager: (pause) Jason. Everyone on this floor has closed one of these. You think the guy with the corner office got there on standard commission?

Manager: It's your call. But I only offer this once.

=> deal_pivot

~ deal_pivot

Jason: (Three triple commissions. A corner office. "Compliance handles compliance.")

Jason: (He took the file.)

do Global.return_from_flashback()

=> END
